    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Blennerhassett is an unincorporated community in Wood County that overlooks the Ohio River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://wvexplorer.com/travel/communities/cities-towns/blennerhassett-west-virginia/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Blennerhassett takes its name from Harman Blennerhassett, an Anglo-Irish immigrant who owned an island in the Ohio River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blennerhassett%2C_West_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Blennerhassett Island Historical State Park is located here, though the ferry to the island departs from Parkersburg.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://wvexplorer.com/travel/communities/cities-towns/blennerhassett-west-virginia/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Blennerhassett Island is the fifth-largest island in the Ohio River, spanning four miles in length.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wvencyclopedia.org/entries/510"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Prehistoric Indians occupied Blennerhassett Island until about sixteen eighty, clearing much of the land to grow corn.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wvencyclopedia.org/entries/510"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Twentieth-century excavations have revealed Blennerhassett Island to be one of the richest archeological sites in the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
